<https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1899-02/page/197> a crm:E22_Man-Made_Object,
        mbdiaries-ontology:Page ;
    rdfs:label "197"^^xsd:string ;
    crm:P129i_is_subject_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/diary/1899-02/document/197> ;
    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493296701/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """ Mine
Yes.
(a) No. (b) I hope for more mental energy and a sense of leisure, but I should be content with present conditions if I could believe there was something worthwhile in suffering.  c . Love and Curiosity and Enjoyment of Nature and Art., 
I should prefer life after death because I have always been very happy and there have been times when I have felt that the happiness, if the conditions continued unchanged, had enough in it to make it last indefinitely. It seems to be only hindering  circumstances  that prevent continuous joy, and I like to dream of vague conditions under which nothing should impede us. My feeling comes really from a sense of having more capacity for enjoyment and happiness and even love than Life has ever yet been able to draw out, or is likely to.
No.
So far as I am aware, I have always felt us I do now, except when I was very much bored trying to live an uncongenial life, and then I imagined that by the time I came to die I should have  had enough , and not want to go on, any more than  one  wants to eat a second dinner at the end of the first. 
I should rather not  know , at least with the risk of the answer being negative. I like to have a sort of fringe of hope at the edge of existence. 
I look forward to Death with interest and a sort of pleasant curiosity, with a hope that somehow everything afterwards """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 197 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1899-02> .

